WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.860
Dependency risk is not limited to the source code of the library.

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:16.280
Build configuration can enable or disable parsers, codecs, crypto backends, network modules, debugging hooks, optional plugins, deprecated compatibility modes, and generated artifacts.

3
00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:22.940
Two teams can use the same library version and still ship meaningfully different behavior because they selected different build options.

4
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:30.580
CMake options, package manager features, platform defaults, compiler settings, and linker behavior should all be reviewed.

5
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:34.680
Optional components should be enabled only when the product actually needs them.

6
00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:44.220
If a library supports multiple backends, the selected backend should be visible in configuration output and documented when it matters to security or support.

7
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:50.500
Hardening policy should remain consistent when third-party code is built as part of the project.

8
00:00:50.600 --> 00:01:02.280
A dependency should not quietly compile with weaker warnings, different language settings, or incompatible runtime assumptions simply because that is the easiest way to make the build pass.

9
00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:07.980
Exceptions may be necessary, but they should be narrow, documented, and reviewed.

10
00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.740
Build logs are a practical control.

11
00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:18.920
They can confirm which dependency was found, which version was selected, which options were enabled, and which compiler and linker settings applied.

12
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:29.180
CI should preserve enough evidence that release reviewers can tell whether the dependency was integrated with the intended configuration rather than whatever settings happened to work locally.

13
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.880
When the configuration is hard to explain, the team should simplify it or document the reason before release.
